('h5', '4.3.4  Experimental protocol'),
('p', "Each experiment is a script that deploys a configuration, verifies the image and environment of the running pod (an early run was found invalid because an image tag had not been built, so verification was added), injects one fault for a fixed window at about 5 requests per second, records the client-side classification of every response, heals the fault and records the recovery. Trials are repeated 5 to 10 times per cell. Raw results are appended to JSON-lines files that are never rewritten, and invalid runs are kept under names that mark them superseded. Statistics (bootstrap 95 percent intervals, Fisher exact test, Mann-Whitney U test) are computed afterwards by analysis/phase14_stats.py."),

('h3', '4.4  System Architecture'),
('p', "Figure 4.4 shows the deployed system in the Kubernetes namespace climate and the monitoring namespace. The user's browser talks to the Flask frontend, which stores users and history in PostgreSQL and calls the inference API over HTTP. The inference API hosts the models, the guard, the golden check, the tier chain and the deadline logic. The ForecastService operator and the failure predictor observe the inference API; Prometheus scrapes every component every 5 seconds; Grafana and Jaeger present metrics and traces; chaos and probe drivers run outside or inside the cluster to inject faults and measure."),
('fig', 'fig_architecture.png', 'Figure 4.4  System architecture', 6.3),
('p', "Figure 4.5 shows the request path inside the inference API. A request starts at the requested tier (LSTM by default). The output of a tier is accepted only if it passes the envelope and invariant checks, the golden-output check and the deadline or queue condition; otherwise the tier is marked suspect (circuit breaker) or shed and the next tier is tried. A background canary every five seconds re-validates suspect tiers so that a repaired model returns to service without a restart."),
('fig', 'fig_tiers.png', 'Figure 4.5  Tiered degradation and guard checks', 6.3),
